// THE DOCUMENT CARRIES RULES, NOT THEIR HISTORY. A dated operator ruling is cited by its
// gunbc.design_ruling row -- `ruling(r:)` renders the row's own reference -- and the row keeps
// when and on whose words; the rule itself is stated only here.
//
// THE CLAIMS ARE gunbc.design_argument ROWS. A section's heading, its bold lead sentence and its
// citation line are rendered from the row (`claim_section`), and a cross-reference to another
// section is rendered from that row's title (`see`), so a claim is worded once and a section is
// cited by symbol. The prose below each lead explains the claim; it never restates it differently.
// Sections carry no numbers: reading order is presentation, and the argument graph is checked on
// its premises in gunbc.design_argument.

// THE HEADINGS OF THE PARTS THAT ARE NOT CLAIMS, named once. A claim's heading is its row's title;
// these four parts have no row, so a consumer that names them (gunbc.contributor_onboarding_path's
// reading order) imports the heading rather than spelling it.
data purpose_heading: String = "Purpose"
data worked_examples_heading: String = "Worked examples"
data ci_contract_heading: String = "Reference: building and checks"
data ledgers_heading: String = "Reference: ledgers"

fn h1(text: String) -> MarkdownBlock { HeadingBlock { level: H1, inlines: [TextInline { text: text }] } }

fn ruling(r: DesignRuling) -> String {
  concat("(ruling ", concat(documentary_ref_symbol_text(reference: r.reference), ")"))
}

fn see(r: DesignRow) -> String {
  concat("*", concat(r.title, "*"))
}

fn cites(r: DesignRow) -> String {
  documentary_ref_symbol_text(reference: r.cite)
}

fn claim_lead(r: DesignRow) -> MarkdownBlock {
  p(text: concat("**", concat(r.proposition.statement, "**")))
}

fn claim_cite(r: DesignRow) -> MarkdownBlock {
  p(text: concat("*Cite as* ", concat(cites(r: r), ".")))
}

fn claim_section(r: DesignRow, body: List<MarkdownBlock>) -> List<MarkdownBlock> {
  concat([h3(text: r.title), claim_lead(r: r)], concat(body, [claim_cite(r: r)]))
}

fn tbl(header: List<String>, rows: List<List<String>>) -> MarkdownBlock {
  TableBlock {
    header: row(cells: map(header, h => cell(text: h))),
    alignments: map(header, h => AlignNone),
    rows: map(rows, r => row(cells: map(r, c => cell(text: c)))),
  }
}

fn preamble_blocks() -> List<MarkdownBlock> {
  [
    h1(text: "gunbc — Design"),
    p(text: "`README.md` and `CLAUDE.md` symlink here; this is the single source of truth. It is a projection of `gunbc.design_document` and `gunbc.design_argument` and is never hand-edited. v1 is the `gunbc` CLI and v2's seed; v2 is active."),
    p(text: "The document is an argument. Each section states one claim, rendered from its `gunbc.design_argument` row, and names the symbol to cite it by; sections carry no numbers, so cite a section by its symbol, never by its position. The argument graph — which claims each claim rests on — is checked in `gunbc.design_argument` (`std.syllogism` `argument_form_is_valid`: every claim reachable from the commitments, acyclic, closed, uniquely identified). That check establishes a well-formed graph over string statements; whether a conclusion follows from its premises remains a judgment. The order of sections is for reading only. The principles apply to this document too."),
    p(text: concat("It carries reasoning only, because it is loaded in full on every turn of every session while ledgers grow without bound. The ledgers live apart: recurring failure modes one class per file under `dag/gunbc/recurring_failure_mode/`, declared rung drops under `gunbc.rung_drop` (combined views of both: `gunbc.instruments.docs_projection_gate` `regen`), and dated operator rulings in `gunbc.design_ruling`, cited below by symbol. A plan's authority is its `.dag` carrier; generated plan markdown whose only consumer is ProjectDocumentation is not a checkout artifact ", concat(ruling(r: project_documentation_projections_are_not_checkout_artifacts), ". Write it on demand (`gunbc.plan_documentation` `plan_documentation_wet`). Plans that still project committed markdown stay linked from the section that governs them."))),
    hr(),
  ]
}

// ── Purpose and method ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

fn purpose_blocks() -> List<MarkdownBlock> {
  [
    h2(text: "Purpose and method"),
    h3(text: purpose_heading),
    p(text: concat("**", concat(goal_safe_efficient_predictable.statement, ".**"))),
    tbl(header: ["Goal", "What it means in practice"], rows: [
      ["**Safe**", "Invalid states and actions are prevented where the model allows it. Remaining failures and uncertainty stay explicit. Every action stays within its authority."],
      ["**Efficient**", "The process does justified work, reuses valid results, keeps no competing definitions, and creates no unnecessary work for authors, reviewers, operators or future maintainers."],
      ["**Predictable**", "Inputs, dependencies, decisions, effects and failure behavior are explicit enough to explain what happens and why. External uncertainty has a defined place in the model."],
    ]),
    p(text: "Predictable does not mean the world is predictable. A network request can have an unknown outcome while the system's response to that unknown is exactly specified. What is predictable is the process, including how it handles uncertainty."),
    h3(text: "How designs are evaluated"),
    p(text: concat("These are choices the project makes, not conclusions derived here. We adopt **", concat(time_is_the_measure.statement, concat("**, including the time asked of everyone who depends on the process: cost is time to run, a silent wrong answer is time to recover paid later at interest, and complexity is time to change. These perspectives overlap — efficiency matters throughout the lifecycle, while safety and predictability constrain how work is performed. And **", concat(agreement_is_temporal.statement, "**, which is why grounding (below) points at shared, time-stable frameworks."))))),
  ]
}

fn method_blocks() -> List<MarkdownBlock> {
  flat_map([
    claim_section(r: method_separate_order_compose, body: [
      p(text: "We **choose** this architecture because it serves the purpose. Four ideas, each answering a different question:"),
      tbl(header: ["Idea", "Question it answers"], rows: [
        ["**First-principles reasoning**", "Which goals, definitions, facts or assumptions justify this decision?"],
        ["**Separation of concerns**", "Which meanings and decisions must stay independently understandable and governable?"],
        ["**Dependency order**", "What must be established before this conclusion or decision is justified?"],
        ["**Explicit composition**", "Where, and under what contract, do separate concerns come together into one process?"],
      ]),
      p(text: "**Dependency order is a partial order, not a staircase.** Two concerns that do not depend on each other have no order between them. A provider's contract and a workflow's waiting budget are independent inputs: both must exist before a request is planned, and neither comes first. **Execution order is a separate fact**: obtain a credential before using it, receive an operation identity before polling it, observe completion before claiming convergence. Those temporal dependencies are modeled inside the process where they hold; they are not the order the architecture is organized by."),
      p(text: "The sources are D. L. Parnas, \"On the Criteria To Be Used in Decomposing Systems into Modules\" (CACM, 1972), which decomposes so that a change to one design decision does not propagate, and E. W. Dijkstra, \"On the role of scientific thought\" (EWD447, 1974), which studies one aspect at a time while staying aware of the whole. Neither implies one universal sequence."),
      p(text: "**A concern** is a meaning, fact, decision or effect that can be understood and governed on its own: it has its own authority, can vary independently, needs its own evidence, or has its own failure conditions."),
      p(text: "**The review question that makes separation observable:** *if this decision changes while its public contract stays stable, which other parts must change, and why?* Two consequences: **equal values are not equal concerns** — seven occurrences of \"30 seconds\" justify asking whether one policy was copied, and do not by themselves establish that all seven are one policy; and **different representations are not competing authorities** — a duration and its rendered wire string `\"600s\"` can be two representations of one fact, and the defect is a wire encoding that independently chooses, alters or duplicates the value."),
      p(text: "**What the method asks of an implementation.** These are obligations the method sets; choosing the architecture does not establish them, and an implementation establishes them where its evidence shows it does."),
      ul(items: [
        li(text: "**Efficient:** define each rule at its authority and reuse its definitions and valid results. Re-evaluate it when changed inputs, elapsed time or a trust boundary requires fresh validation, and reuse evidence only while its scope and validity conditions hold. Concerns with no logical dependency can be understood independently; their executions may overlap when their effects, temporal requirements and resource constraints permit it."),
        li(text: "**Safe:** reject an invalid state at the earliest boundary that has the information and authority to decide it. Composition owns the checks that depend on several concerns. Keep later checks where changing state or a trust boundary requires them."),
        li(text: "**Predictable:** declare the dependencies needed to explain behavior and change impact, establish where the implementation respects that model, and make missing or uncertain dependencies explicit."),
      ]),
      p(text: "**Applying it: five questions.** These explain the method; they are not a fixed layer model, and an example need not have an interesting answer to every one."),
      ol(items: [
        li(text: "**Why does this process exist?** What result do we want, and which outcomes are unacceptable?"),
        li(text: "**What are its distinct concerns?** Which meanings, facts, choices and effects need separate treatment?"),
        li(text: "**What does each concern depend on?** Who supplies its inputs and governs its decisions?"),
        li(text: "**How do the parts compose into a process?** Where are compatibility, authority and ordering established?"),
        li(text: "**How do we establish and preserve correctness?** What is guaranteed, what is observed, and what remains a judgment?"),
      ]),
    ]),
    claim_section(r: atomic_concern, body: [
      p(text: "An atom is not a field, a type, a function or a file. Questions that suggest a part hides more than one responsibility — investigate them; none is an automatic reason to split:"),
      ul(items: [
        li(text: "Do its decisions have different authorities?"),
        li(text: "Can they vary independently?"),
        li(text: "Do they need different evidence?"),
        li(text: "Do they have different failure or refusal conditions? One coherent operation can have several refusal conditions; different errors do not by themselves mean different responsibilities."),
        li(text: "Can they disagree without either being malformed?"),
      ]),
      p(text: "Mathematical concepts, upstream contracts and opaque interfaces can supply an atomic boundary; none establishes atomicity by itself. The test for any further split is *what independently meaningful decision would it expose?* — otherwise \"atomic\" becomes an invitation to add machinery forever. **Atomic to its consumer:** a concern may be atomic where it is used while its internal structure is owned elsewhere. An application consumes a duration; it does not reimplement the algebra of quantities."),
    ]),
    claim_section(r: explicit_composition, body: [
      p(text: "A planner that combines a provider limit, a workflow policy and a current observation is doing real, necessary work: deciding whether those inputs admit an action. It receives each independently owned decision explicitly and never quietly invents one. Bringing several concerns together is not fusion; fusion is a composition that hides or originates the decisions it should receive."),
    ]),
    claim_section(r: grounding_is_intersubjective, body: [
      p(text: concat("Because time is the one value we assume is shared and agreement holds only on what is stable over time, a fact's authority is a framework others can check, not a name we coined. ", concat(see(r: external_upstreams), " and the substrate below apply this."))),
    ]),
    claim_section(r: reduce_to_physics, body: [
      p(text: "This is why the corpus models the universal frameworks and real upstreams rather than re-coining them: a nickname is convention standing where necessity was available. Safety has its own measure, used throughout: how far each error class has climbed from runtime harm toward structural impossibility."),
    ]),
  ], bs => bs)
}

// ── Worked examples ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

fn worked_examples_blocks() -> List<MarkdownBlock> {
  [
    h2(text: worked_examples_heading),
    p(text: "Two problems sorted by the method, read at revision `8d22701ad6ca`. The findings are dated, scoped specimens; fixing them is tracked by their owners and is not a precondition of this document. The acceptance question for the whole document is the one these examples answer: *can someone take an unfamiliar complex problem, identify its atomic concerns and dependencies, compose them into a process, and explain both what the result guarantees and what remains uncertain?*"),
    h3(text: "Integers: meaning, operations, constraints, realization"),
    p(text: "`std.integer` separates five things a typical language fuses into `int`:"),
    tbl(header: ["Concern", "Where", "What it is"], rows: [
      ["What an integer **means**", "`std.integer::Int` (`NonNegative { n: Nat }` or `Negative { pred: Nat }`); `UInt = Nat`", "the mathematical value: one zero, no width"],
      ["How naturals and integers **relate**", "`std.integer::nat_to_int`", "a declared injection and the only implicit route between the two types (total, exact, pure, commuting with add, mul and compare); the reverse is a checked crossing, `std.checked_arithmetic::checked_int_to_nat`"],
      ["Which **operations** exist", "`std.integer::int_ordered_ring`", "an algebra over the type, not the type itself"],
      ["Which **machine constraint** applies", "`UInt8 = Compose<UInt, MachineWidth<8>>` … `UInt128`", "width as its own axis: five widths across the signed and unsigned families give ten aliases of one composition, not ten independently specified numeric models"],
      ["How a host **realizes** them", "`kernel_grounding_rows`", "a separate fact; the mathematical embedding is never read off it"],
    ]),
    p(text: concat("**The limit, stated in the model itself.** `std.integer` records that `UInt8` is still phantom today: transparent to `Int` at a function parameter, so a bare `Int` reaches a `UInt8` parameter unchecked — a floor receipt observed `256` crossing a `UInt8` parameter in `test.claim.machine_word_witness`. The intended model is now decided ", concat(ruling(r: machine_width_composition_has_an_intersection_carrier), ": `Compose<UInt, MachineWidth<N>>` denotes an intersection — the algebra value restricted to the machine interval, with the width retained in its type — not a transparent alias of `UInt`. Recording that ruling claims no runtime or emitted-width guarantee: the range is enforced only once the checked width mint and its target realization land together. **A good model expresses the intended separation; a guarantee requires the production path to enforce it.** A declaration's name establishes nothing on its own."))),
    h3(text: "A Google operation within a budget"),
    p(text: "**The process:** perform a Google IAM operation within the caller's time budget, using a credential whose validity is known, and report what actually happened."),
    p(text: "**1. Why it exists, and what is unacceptable.** We want the operation done, or a typed account of why not. Unacceptable: admitting a new attempt after its local budget is exhausted or with a credential that fails the applicable admission checks; waiting past the remaining budget (the transport honors the admitted budget during execution — checking it just before sending is necessary, not sufficient); claiming success that was not observed; reissuing a non-idempotent mutation because a response was lost; a refusal that arrives only as an HTTP 400 when it could have been refused before sending. A local deadline does not stop an operation the remote side has already accepted — gRPC documents that `DEADLINE_EXCEEDED` can be returned when a state-changing operation completed — so the obligation is to bound the local wait and preserve uncertainty about any request that may already have taken effect."),
    p(text: "**2. The distinct concerns, and who governs each.** The repair for a value \"slammed into application code\" follows the value's meaning and authority, not its spelling, and does not mean moving every number into the Google model."),
    tbl(header: ["Concern", "Authority"], rows: [
      ["What a duration is: quantity, unit, scale", "the shared quantity model, `std.measure::Second` (`Measure<Time, One, Nat>`)"],
      ["What an instant is, and its clock domain", "the clock and time model"],
      ["Google's request fields and their wire encoding", "that Google API's contract (protobuf `Duration` JSON form, e.g. `\"600s\"`)"],
      ["Google's default and its conditional limits", "the cited upstream contract, `extdeps.cloud.gcp.iam`"],
      ["How long this workflow wants the credential to live", "workflow policy"],
      ["How long this workflow is willing to wait", "workflow policy"],
      ["When the credential actually expires", "an observation: Google's response (`expireTime`)"],
      ["How much budget remains for the next attempt", "derived execution state"],
      ["Rendering and enforcing a transport deadline", "transport realization (`extdeps.http.client`, the REST transport)"],
      ["Whether the remote work completed", "response or readback evidence"],
      ["Whether another attempt is permitted", "the operation's retry semantics plus retry policy"],
    ]),
    p(text: "**3. Dependencies, and what the tree does today.**"),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "**The JWKS fetch budget is workflow policy, mis-typed.** `gunbc.auth.oidc_id_token_verification::google_oidc_jwks_fetch_max_seconds` is the string `\"10\"`, passed to `extdeps.http.client` `GetWithin`, which renders it as curl's `--max-time`. The endpoint is Google's; the ten seconds is the verifier's policy; the string form belongs only at the transport boundary — moving it into the Google model would fuse them again. Unit correctness, admissible range and transport sentinel behavior are three obligations: `std.measure::Second` admits zero, and to curl a zero timeout means no timeout, so retyping to `Second` establishes only the first. While `GetWithin` takes `max_seconds: NonEmptyStr`, any caller can bypass a stronger type."),
      li(text: "**The JWKS cache reuses keys on a local lifetime without the origin's freshness evidence.** Google directs verifiers to use the key response's `Cache-Control` to decide when to fetch again. `jwks_cache_decode` marks an entry fresh when the local age is below `google_oidc_jwks_cache_ttl_seconds` (3600), and the cache record keeps only `fetched_at` and `body`. The repair separates what the response permits (its directives and age — an observation), what the verifier is willing to retain (policy; a one-hour cap can be legitimate), and the reuse decision (their composition). The origin's response is not just a second duration: its directives can require validation or prohibit storage, and freshness is computed against the response's current age (RFC 9111). A local cap can shorten a permitted freshness interval; it cannot authorize reuse that requires validation or is prohibited."),
      li(text: "**The token-lifetime model flattens a conditional fact into a scalar.** Google documents a default and an ordinary maximum of one hour; up to twelve hours requires the service account to be allowed by the organization-policy constraint `constraints/iam.allowServiceAccountCredentialLifetimeExtension`, and the response carries the actual `expireTime`. `extdeps.cloud.gcp.iam::default_token_lifetime` holds `default_seconds: 3600, max_seconds: 43200` with no representation of that condition, the inspected production request paths do not consume it, and `GenerateAccessToken` defaults its own input to the string `\"3600s\"`. The upstream model owes the provider default, the ordinary limit, the conditional extended limit and the condition for a named account; wiring the existing record into production would not be enough. A workflow may still deliberately cap at one hour."),
      li(text: "**`optional_impersonation` encodes the wire form in policy code and drops the observation.** `gunbc.auth.optional_impersonation` takes `lifetime_seconds: Int where range(min: 1, max: 3600)` — a retired where-refinement restating the ordinary limit — builds the wire value with `concat(to_string(lifetime_seconds), \"s\")`, and returns the token while discarding `expire_time`. The workflow chooses a requested lifetime, the API model owns the legal range and the encoding, and any downstream claim that the credential is valid consumes the returned expiry. A checked constructor can refuse a request that breaks the modeled rule before sending; it cannot guarantee Google accepts it, and an observed expiry establishes a lifetime condition, not every aspect of validity."),
      li(text: "**\"Poll budget ≤ token lifetime\" relates two configured numbers.** `gunbc.auth.gcp_operation_wait::gcp_operation_budget_fits_actuation_allowance` checks 120 s ≤ 600 s. The wait derives 24 ticks from a 5 s cadence and `operation_wait_step` gives up only after reading at zero, so a running operation sees 25 reads and 24 sleeps; RPC time, scheduling delay and the credential's prior use are not deducted, and the observed expiry is not consumed. It therefore bounds no elapsed time, even for one wait: elapsed wait is sleep time plus RPC time plus other overhead, and the pacing budget bounds only the first. What it does well: the relation is named and stated once, and an unreadable operation is a typed outcome (`OperationWaitUnreadable`) that never reissues the create."),
      li(text: "**A candidate family: the approval-submit deadline.** The `submit_timeout` passed to `approval_file_stored_request` is 30 s at seven sites — inline in `approved_gcp_access`, `branch_run_grant`, `printer_execution_hold` and `printer_operator_approval`, and as the rows `gcp_iam_converge_submit_timeout`, `srv1_root_mutation_submit_timeout` and `microvm_runner_group_submit_timeout`. Equal values are a reason to ask, not a finding. If the seven are one decision (how long any caller waits for the broker to accept a submission), its ownership is consolidated; callers may still pass the shared policy explicitly, including a tighter remaining budget, because one authority does not require an implicit dependency. The review question decides it: if the broker's admission latency changed, would all seven change together?"),
    ]),
    p(text: "**4. Composition, made visible.** Three stages, each consuming what the previous one observed (illustrative shape, not checked syntax):"),
    CodeBlock { code: "// 1. Acquire the credential, keeping its returned expiry and identity.\ntoken_request = iam.admit_token_request(provider_contract, observed_account_policy,\n                    requested_lifetime: workflow.requested_lifetime)?        // refuses before send\ncredential    = execute_within(token_request, task_budget)?                  // Credential { secret, expires_at, principal }\n\n// 2. Admit and execute the operation, consuming that credential and the REMAINING budget.\nop_request    = iam.admit_operation(intent, credential,\n                    now_wall: clock.wall_now(), now_mono: clock.monotonic_now(),\n                    remaining: task_budget.remaining(now_mono))?             // refuses if expiry or budget does not admit it\noutcome       = transport.execute(op_request)                                // honors the admitted budget during execution\n\n// 3. Interpret or observe the outcome.\nmatch outcome {\n  Answered(response)       => iam.decode_operation(response)                 // keeps the accepted operation identity\n  ProvenNotSent(reason)    => RefusedBeforeSend(reason)\n  DeliveryUncertain(cause) => OutcomeUnknown(cause)   // a lost response does not establish whether the remote operation succeeded\n}\n// OutcomeUnknown resolves only by later observation (readback), never by re-issuing the mutation." },
    p(text: "The composition owes: the original task deadline survives retries and is never renewed; each attempt gets only the remaining admitted budget, re-checked immediately before sending; encoding to the wire form is an explicit checked conversion; a timeout keeps the uncertainty about effects whenever the transport cannot establish what happened; and retrying follows the operation's real retry semantics. Clock domains stay explicit at the handoff: `expires_at` is a wall-clock instant from Google and the task deadline is monotonic, so composing them needs a clock observation and a stated uncertainty assumption such as bounded skew. There is not one \"timeout\": Google's client libraries distinguish the per-RPC timeout, the retry timeout (checked before starting a retry, so it does not bound an attempt already running) and the polling timeout."),
    p(text: "**5. How the organization is judged.** A reviewer criterion says which question to ask; discriminating cases show whether the decomposition changed what the system can safely do. The result is a list of specific obligations, established guarantees and remaining gaps — never a score from counting populated rows or registered mechanisms."),
    tbl(header: ["Probe", "What the organized model should establish"], rows: [
      ["Change the workflow's waiting budget", "the provider contract keeps its meaning"],
      ["Change the provider's accepted range", "the request boundary consumes the revised contract, with no restated copy"],
      ["Pass a value in the wrong unit", "the boundary refuses, or performs an explicit checked conversion"],
      ["Encode a value outside the transport's range", "no wrapping or silent truncation"],
      ["Spend the budget while queued or between retries", "the next send is refused"],
      ["Slow RPCs under a nominally small polling budget", "any claimed elapsed-time guarantee accounts for request time, not just sleep ticks"],
      ["Lose the response after a possible mutation", "the outcome stays uncertain until the protocol allows a stronger conclusion"],
      ["A response lost after an accepted effect", "later observation can establish completion without issuing the mutation again"],
      ["Bypass the checked planner", "the production boundary still refuses, or the stated guarantee is lowered to match"],
      ["Valid request, controlled successful provider responses", "the admitted request reaches the intended production route and produces the expected result; a valid request alone cannot guarantee remote success"],
    ]),
  ]
}

// ── Meaning and authority ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

fn meaning_blocks() -> List<MarkdownBlock> {
  concat([h2(text: "Meaning and authority")], flat_map([
    claim_section(r: minimize_redundancy, body: [
      p(text: "Redundant work defers cost onto a later fixer or into a process destined to be thrown out; refusing it is refusing to spend someone's future time to buy the author's present convenience. It is removed along two directions of one move:"),
      ul(items: [
        li(text: "**Horizontal — one concept, every scale and breadth.** Model a concept once and derive every use; at the right layer nanosecond memoization and broad infra deployment are the same concept. *e.g.* the integer widths in the worked example: one axis, not ten types."),
        li(text: "**Deep — every concept decomposed to grounded atoms.** Nothing is opaque that is not genuinely atomic. The move is `decompress → map → reduce`: reveal the structure the source names, map each part onto the concept that already exists (search the concept graph first), reduce duplicates. A `String` leaf hiding named parts is anemic modeling. *e.g.* `\"LGA4926\"` → `CpuSocket { package: LandGridArray, contact_count: 4926 }`, the number a grounded `Int` rather than a fresh enum."),
      ]),
      p(text: "The test that an edit *reduced* redundancy rather than moving it: **net concepts must not grow by re-invention.** Decomposing a leaf by minting a fresh authority for a concept that already exists is a failed decomposition."),
    ]),
    claim_section(r: single_authority, body: [
      p(text: "The recurring violation is **nicknaming — a second name for one concept** — which duplicates work at the meaning layer and again in everything generated from it. A fork is always consolidated later, so it is a correctness concern, not a style one. One definition may have several realizations, generated projections and enforcement points; none of those is a competing authority. Its dual is the **meaning fork**: within one naming surface and one declared version or epoch, holding a name constant may not silently change obligations, quality floor, refusal behavior, billing consequence or remedy. The same spelling in distinct scopes, or across a declared version transition, is legitimate; a materially different contract needs a materially different name."),
      p(text: "Two corollaries. The import graph's only structural law is **acyclicity**: the `std`/`extdeps`/`compiler`/`workflow` folders are browsing conventions, not a direction rule. And a fact's home is its *layer of meaning*, not its file."),
      p(text: concat("**The name-to-path mapping is a single authority too, and it runs one way.** Module `a.b.c` is stored at `<root(a)>/b/c.dag`, where `root(v2)` is `src/v2` and `root(a)` is `dag/a` for every other top-level namespace: paths follow names, never the reverse. A location that must be read out of a file is a second authority for one fact, and makes finding one module cost reading all of them. Which namespaces exist is declared top-down in `gunbc.source_layout`, and a file outside that layout is not compilable. The rule fixes where a module is stored, not which way imports point. A crossing between the trees is admitted when both namespaces have a row in that layout, either way, and refused when either has none (`v2.std.cross_tree.import_model` `cross_tree_import_admitted`) ", concat(ruling(r: cross_tree_admission_is_namespace_membership), ", enforced through the storage lookup."))),
      p(text: concat("**Cite the symbol, not the position.** A `file:line` pointer, or a section number, is a second, positional naming scheme that any edit above it silently invalidates. Name the module and symbol, spelled `module.path::decl` (`std.decl_ref` `declaration_ref_display_key`); a position is carried only where no symbol exists (a generated artifact, a data row, a line inside a blob), and then beside the symbol, never as the citation. A stale symbol is decidable from the `Node` tree; a position is not reachable from it at all. This document's own sections are cited the same way, by their `gunbc.design_argument` rows. When a citation's target moves, every citation moves with it in the same change; ", concat(see(r: source_annotations), " says how a comment's citations are checked."))),
      p(text: concat("**A shared secret has one admission too.** `gunbc.fleet.fleet_ssh_access` `fleet_authorized_keys` admits the fleet roster once, and every install route that completes an install and runs sshd on the resulting system seeds authorized_keys from that one roster; no route may hand-spell a second key list or name a single machine's row. The PXE autoinstall and host-reconcile routes already consumed it; the OCI Always Free launch is a sibling under this one rule, witnessed to carry the roster and red where the route omits it. The diskless runner-host boot medium is now covered by this same rule: `autoinstall.ssh` is a target-installer feature (Subiquity writes it under `/target` at postinstall), so a rendered autoinstall ssh section is not evidence of live authorization on a session that never completes an install or starts sshd; the route is closed instead by the runner_host_medium boot stage, which seeds the live session's own authorized_keys from the roster and is witnessed over the rendered live stage ", ruling(r: fleet_authorized_keys_on_every_install_route), ".")),
    ]),
    claim_section(r: interface_realization_policy, body: [
      p(text: "`extdeps/` owns a dependency's *interface shape*; *transport* (shell, REST, SDK) is one of N handlers bound to it; *business policy* (which base ref, which flag, how long to wait) is a workflow fact. Policy has leaked when an argv carries a literal it should receive as a parameter; transport has fused when an operation is forked per transport. The dispatch that selects a realization is itself realization, so it sits peripheral. The Google example above is this claim worked through one process."),
      p(text: "*e.g.* one vendor concept once forked three ways by rigor — `CpuVendor`/`DramManufacturer` closed enums beside a stringly `GpuFacts.vendor` — dissolved into one generic `Vendor<Domain>` whose cited company entities live one file each in `extdeps/vendor/`. Likewise `dag/std/os.dag` projects per-vendor rows with no `match`, while the rows and the product-to-vendor dispatch live in `extdeps/os/`."),
    ]),
  ], bs => bs))
}

// ── Facts and evidence ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

fn facts_blocks() -> List<MarkdownBlock> {
  concat([h2(text: "Facts and evidence")], flat_map([
    claim_section(r: external_upstreams, body: [
      p(text: "Model the accepted universal frameworks (classical logic, set theory, algebra) faithfully, and in `extdeps/` the real upstream: cite the source, keep its real names, declare its version, model what the API actually returns — including its conditions, as the token-lifetime specimen shows. One concept, one authority unifies a shared interface or formal shape; it does not merge independently governed entities that inhabit it. A generic hub may define agnostic shapes but may not enumerate concrete products, dispatch among them, carry product version rows, or store consumer coverage state. A missing observation is a downstream coverage obligation, never an `Unobserved` property inside the upstream module."),
      CodeBlock { code: "extdeps.whatwg.html_navigation        shared standard\nextdeps.browser.chromium              Chromium implementation\nextdeps.browser.google_chrome         Chrome distribution\nextdeps.automation.playwright         automation dependency\ngunbc.served_surface_browser_support  support policy and evidence join" },
      p(text: "A Chrome change must not require editing Safari’s module. Adding Opera must not widen a generic browser-product enum."),
    ]),
    claim_section(r: epistemic_humility, body: [
      p(text: concat(see(r: guarantee_ladder), concat(" governs what the compiler can guarantee about a program; this governs what the corpus may assert about the world. The obligation is symmetric ", concat(ruling(r: epistemic_humility_is_symmetric), ":")))),
      ul(items: [
        li(text: "**Do not assert as deduced what is only inferred.** An inference is a bet: typed as a bet, neither deleted nor promoted. A reader who consumes it as fact is the defect."),
        li(text: concat("**Do not over-generalize a prohibition.** Forbidding more than the evidence supports costs opportunities nobody sees, so it looks like rigor. This is not ", concat(see(r: fail_closed), "'s never-trap (claiming construction for an undecidable class); it is forbidding more of the world than the cited variables support."))),
        li(text: "**Do assert what you are confident in.** A fact declined is a wall that does not exist. Under-asserting is not humility."),
      ]),
      p(text: "The homes: `extdeps.external_authority` `CitedFigureStanding` = `CitedToAuthority { authority }` | `TranscribedUncited { read_obligation }` carries a figure's evidentiary standing in its type, an uncited figure naming what would ground it; and `strategy.node_rental` `SupplierHypothesis`, whose only arm answering true requires a cited discriminator. The cautionary specimen: `product.node_power_envelope` `altra_two_dpc_ceilings` were self-declared `TranscribedUncited`, and `effective_data_rate` consumed them as a wall — a typed bet consumed as deduced fact, whatever the sources later say."),
    ]),
  ], bs => bs))
}

// ── Representation and checking ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

fn representation_blocks() -> List<MarkdownBlock> {
  concat([h2(text: "Representation and checking")], flat_map([
    claim_section(r: closed_grounded_substrate, body: [
      p(text: "A program is a dependency graph over two primitives (`Node` + `Edge`) and a closed vocabulary of 6 connectives and 6 behaviors — `Value`, `Transform`, `Branch`, `Loop`, `Bind`, `Match`. Surface syntax is sugar that adds no power. Execution is **bounded and forward** (cyclic relations via acyclic encodings, recursion as sugar over `Loop`), so decidability and termination fall out rather than being separately proved."),
      p(text: "In a closed system **a heuristic is never necessary**: the richer source always exists or can be written. Because the substrate is closed, emission, ingestion and coercion are **one** total decision procedure run in different directions — N models, not N×M adapters, every refusal a located, typed mismatch."),
      ul(items: [
        li(text: "*e.g.* `dag/std/algebra.dag` derives `Int.add` from `Int` inhabiting a ring; termination is *checked, not discovered* — `DescentEvidence = Strict | NonIncreasing | DescentUnknown` inhabits a `BoundedLattice` with bottom = fail-closed."),
        li(text: "*e.g.* **one grammar, read in both directions.** Ingest selects a production forward to fold surface syntax into a core `Node`; emit selects from the *same* rows backward, so a new target language is rows in `extdeps/languages/`, never an edit to the fold. Coercion is the same move sideways: a homomorphism check."),
      ]),
      p(text: concat("**Coercion is the implicit-safe subset of declared conversions** ", concat(ruling(r: coercion_is_the_implicit_safe_subset), ". An implicit route must be total, exact, semantics-preserving, unique, pure and consent-free; a phase that changes interpretation, needs proof, may lose information, needs a policy or has competing routes refuses implicit coercion, and the author names a conversion plan. Every phase stays represented in the typed program. Target realization may refuse to realize a plan but never decides whether a conversion is legal, so a target's cast table is not the source language's semantics. *e.g.* `Bool` does not implicitly inhabit `Int`; `true as Int` refuses unless it names a declared route such as `bool_indicator`; `std.integer::nat_to_int` is the declared route from `Nat`."))),
      p(text: concat("Where-refinements are retired in favour of checked constructors returning `Optional` ", concat(ruling(r: where_refinements_retire_for_checked_constructors), "."))),
      p(text: concat("**Text has two representations of one type, and every crossing goes through the declared unfold or refuses** ", concat(ruling(r: text_crossings_go_through_the_unfold), ". `v2.std.text` `String` is `FreeMonoid<Char>`; the kernel `String` is host text; neither is a nickname for the other. The only route between them is the Unicode scalar unfold, `gunbc.structural_realization_bindings` `literal_homomorphism_rows` `UnicodeScalarSequenceUnfold`. Type compatibility keys on the exact declaration a position resolves to, never its leaf name; the unfold is not a property of literals, so a non-literal value at a crossing takes the same route or refuses; and the untranslated crossings inside the emitted compiler are measured and dispositioned under `gunbc.rung_drop.text_boundary_identity_wall`, whose standing follows the measurement."))),
    ]),
    claim_section(r: source_annotations, body: [
      p(text: "Annotation syntax goes through an annotation-specific lexical channel and may not produce a semantic token or binding; adding, deleting or moving an annotation cannot alter any occurrence identity, semantic graph, resolution, semantic hash or target bytes, and semantic passes see only the annotation-erased projection. A comment attaches to its gap: the innermost retained source region containing it (the file when none does) and that region's direct children on either side, so a comment anywhere in a `.dag` file has a place and none refuses (`std.source_annotation`)."),
      p(text: "**Comments are best-effort, never guaranteed documentation.** Every captured annotation is modeling debt — prose the executable model does not yet represent — and the project reserves the right to delete any comment at any time. Nothing may depend on a comment for correctness, and a reader may not treat one as evidence that a machine claim holds, since no `Accepted` program can read it. Nothing reads its prose, so deleting a comment is always a legal edit. What a comment may carry that IS checked is a marked reference, `@ref(module.path::decl)` or `@ref(module.path::decl::field)` (grammar and the one extractor: `std.annotation_reference`; a literal spelling is escaped `\\@ref(`): the required job runs `v2.cli.reference_check` `check_references` over the governed trees, so renaming or deleting a declaration surfaces every comment still citing it, at its location, instead of leaving it stale. Unmarked text is never a reference. Resolution establishes that the cited declaration exists and is unique, not that the sentence around it is true; a comment that cites by position (a section number, a line) rather than by marker is unchecked, and is the first candidate for deletion."),
      p(text: "**Prose is not forbidden; unclassified prose is.** An invariant, receipt, event, ruling, citation, status, count or dissolution condition belongs in a typed carrier, and a `String` declaration whose only purpose is commentary is misplaced or dead data. This was measured: making `//` a parse error made comment syntax unwritable but not commentary, which moved into `data …: String` rows indistinguishable from program data. `//` is the explicit quarantine boundary. An annotation may record irreducible rationale for *why* a construction has its shape; it must not restate what the declaration says."),
    ]),
  ], bs => bs))
}

// ── Composition and execution ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

fn composition_blocks() -> List<MarkdownBlock> {
  concat([h2(text: "Composition and execution")], flat_map([
    claim_section(r: demand_before_materialization, body: [
      p(text: "The order of questions, whose structural authority is `std.materialization_ladder`:"),
      ol(items: [
        li(text: "**Is the repetition authored duplication?** When several demands have a shared-state least common ancestor, carry, rewire or share the first value. Caching a later request only makes redundant work cheap and suppresses the signal that would rank it for deletion."),
        li(text: "**Is there a reuse obligation at all?** One undeclared pure demand may recompute. Isolated consumers, declared replay, or unbounded future siblings instead create one reuse obligation at their least common visible ancestor."),
        li(text: "**Does a provider discharge it?** One computation identity joins those demands to a provider whose scope reaches that ancestor, whose coverage includes the identity, whose key represents every declared input the result depends on, and whose retention spans the obligated lifetime; otherwise refuse with a typed cause."),
        li(text: "**Only then, the realization.** Demand minimization decides whether another production may exist, and cache purity decides whether a materialized value denotes the same fact. After both hold, unavoidable recurrence may explicitly recompute below the cost floor, and a provider is an optimization only when measured total serving cost is below recomputation."),
      ]),
    ]),
    claim_section(r: selection_precedes_convergence, body: [
      p(text: "A convergence primitive (`std.goal_assessment`, `ensure`) assesses a caller-supplied goal against an independent observation, or decides Noop/Apply/Refuse over a caller-supplied requirement with independent readback. Choosing inside convergence is the conflation forbidden here; the selection authority sits before those folds, in `std.decision`."),
      p(text: "**Pareto assessment is not realization selection.** `std.pareto` computes dominance and the frontier: axis identities rather than positions, per-axis orientation, interval readings, `Incomparable` distinct from `DominanceUndecided`, and refusal on missing or duplicated funded-axis readings. A front of two is not a winner. Realization selection is the policy-bound operation that produces one desired realization; it **consumes** `std.pareto` and must not redeclare its vocabulary — a second decision algebra is a fork."),
      p(text: "The result is scoped, never `Optimal`: `SelectedWithin` binds a `SelectionReceipt` (subject, candidate-field identity and standing, constraint authority, funded axes, evidence snapshot, Pareto survivors, policy authority, selected identity). A change to any of those is a **different decision**, so a stale choice is detectable. The other outcomes:"),
      ul(items: [
        li(text: "Unresolved evidence is `SelectionNeedsEvidence`."),
        li(text: "Two survivors without a policy is `SelectionNeedsPolicy`."),
        li(text: "A candidate violating a hard constraint is excluded *before* Pareto, never ranked."),
        li(text: "An incomplete subject — required (objective-term × decision-variable) cells are an identity join, and terms drop only with order preservation — refuses: absence is not irrelevance, and `SelectedWithin` may not mint over an incomplete closure."),
      ]),
      p(text: concat("Ranking work on a funded multi-axis field **is** this law applied to authorship. `std.decision` is consumed (`product.fleet_operating_point` `fleet_operating_point_selection`), so the decision domain of ", concat(see(r: conformance), " names it; that domain checks inhabitance only, and the hard laws above stay with this claim's reviewer. The effectful cycle after selection — admission, actuation with uncertain completion, independent observation — has no consumed home yet (first consumer: the fleet admission spine); a receipt cannot substitute for readback, and an actuation return cannot mint convergence. The Google example's third stage is that cycle in miniature."))),
    ]),
  ], bs => bs))
}

// ── Guarantees ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

fn guarantees_blocks() -> List<MarkdownBlock> {
  concat([h2(text: "Guarantees")], flat_map([
    claim_section(r: guarantee_ladder, body: [
      p(text: "Safety is the reduction of the state space in which a program can silently do the wrong thing — not the presence of diagnostics or the absence of crashes. The rungs:"),
      ol(items: [
        li(text: "**mitigatable** — the failure occurs; harm is contained by total operations, typed outcomes, bounds, rollback, isolation."),
        li(text: "**mechanically preventable** — a generated test, lens or gate reliably exposes and blocks it, but the invalid state remains writable and safety depends on that mechanism executing and staying enrolled."),
        li(text: "**structurally guaranteed** — the source can still describe the invalid state, but no `Accepted` program contains it: the compiler derives a proof or refusal from modeled structure."),
        li(text: "**structurally impossible** — the invalid state has no constructor in the canonical model."),
      ]),
      p(text: "Higher subsumes lower: construction over proof, proof over validation, validation over mitigation. **Silent wrongness is not a rung**: it is below the ladder and forbidden outright. Beside the ladder, deliberately not a fifth rung, is **outside the modeled guarantee** — external reality, undecidable properties, undeclared intent — observed, refused or mitigated at a declared boundary, never fabricated, so that not modeling something cannot masquerade as a weak implementation."),
      p(text: "**Before writing a top-rung check, ask whether its RED is authorable.** A check whose forbidden state is expressible nowhere it runs is a decoration, green by construction and later cited as coverage. A state unrepresentable in the accepted corpus is often still writable as fixture source handed to the compiler, and then declining that red is specification-without-execution; where no harness can express it, the missing harness is the next-rung trigger."),
      p(text: concat("Every class has an **attainable ceiling, derived rather than aspirational**: a decidable, fully modeled class may reach structural impossibility; a decidable class missing its authority is ", concat(see(r: fail_closed), "'s wall after grounding; a class blocked on a language capability names it as its trigger; an undecidable property remains a ratchet or validator; an external fact remains a boundary obligation. **Below ceiling is a correctness gap, never optional elegance.**"))),
      p(text: "Four meta-obligations make the ladder operational:"),
      ol(items: [
        li(text: "**Rung honesty, at a declared subject grain.** The reported rung equals the rung established by executed evidence — a discriminating RED refused on the real acceptance path plus an accepted positive control. Source→interpretation, source→each emission target and carrier→carrier are separate paths, and a class's rung is the **minimum across its in-scope paths**. A type name, diagnostic variant, inert lens or plan establishes nothing. **Rung inflation** is worse than sitting low, because an inflated class never ranks for climbing."),
        li(text: "**No untracked stall.** A class below ceiling names its next-rung trigger, separating *cannot climb further* from *can climb after one grounding* from *can climb now but unbuilt*. Only the first is permanent."),
        li(text: "**No silent regression.** A rung may be lowered only by declaring previous rung, temporary rung, reason, bounded population and restoration trigger. **The trigger names the CAPABILITY restored**, because a drop is retired by its trigger and nothing else; a trigger naming an artifact states what the artifact must be sufficient for. The review tell is a grain mismatch between loss and trigger: plural loss with singular trigger, corpus loss with per-module trigger."),
        li(text: "**Dissolution on climb — production handling only, never the evidence.** A climb deletes the lower-rung production machinery it obsoletes, but the discriminating RED and positive control **stay enrolled**; an expecting-red probe that greens when its wall lands becomes a permanent regression control."),
      ]),
      p(text: "At a service boundary rung honesty is commercial: an opaque dimension needs a falsifiable quality floor with a named consequence (billing, remedy or refusal), or the claim is marketing. Below-floor delivery refuses or discharges the remedy; materially different delivery is its own named, priced product; losing the ability to verify a floor is a declared drop that may force refusal and never licenses silent below-floor delivery."),
      p(text: "Every newly discovered error class — incident, review finding, runtime exception, falsifier divergence — files or updates one row under `dag/gunbc/recurring_failure_mode/` (`gunbc.recurring_failure_mode`): invalid state, harm, distinguishing facts, rung found at, ceiling with reason, next trigger. Declared drops are rostered in `gunbc.rung_drop`. The two are separate because they are appended separately, and this document names neither population so that an append never regenerates it."),
      p(text: "**The floor first, the differentiator above it.** gunbc must hold the ordinary compiler floor — names resolve, applications bind in exact bijection, values inhabit declared types, fields exist, closed variants eliminate exhaustively — and no higher capability compensates a failure there. Above it, because the substrate carries causal, cardinality, algebraic, effect, ownership, cost and realization facts, the ladder reaches classes ordinary compilers leave to tests and postmortems: an empty collection into a nonempty consumer, recursion without descent proof, a non-idempotent effect under retry, a blown complexity bound, a realization that does not preserve behavior."),
      p(text: "The promise is **not** that every property becomes impossible, but that every modeled class climbs to its highest honest rung with ceiling and residual risk explicit. The compiler does not invent intent, prove unmodeled reality or lift arbitrary predicates to proof, and a brand or `Validated<T>` is cosmetic until construction enforces it — the `UInt8` example is that limit in the corpus today. Climb plan: → [compiler-guarantee recovery gap analysis](docs/plans/compiler-guarantee-recovery-gap-analysis.md)."),
    ]),
    claim_section(r: fail_closed, body: [
      p(text: "Every path succeeds fully or fails with a typed, located diagnostic, and no plausible output is fabricated. Relax toward application-layer leniency only under protest, and lean to infra so others can build on your work. A check restating a constraint the model already carries is a second representation of it; the tell that validation stood where construction was available is that it can be satisfied by editing the *declaration* while the realization still lies."),
      p(text: "Construction works only where membership is **decidable**, so every class is a *wall now* (decidable and grounded), a *wall after grounding* (decidable, awaiting its authority), or a *ratchet forever* (undecidable — optimality, by Rice). The word **never is the trap**: it lets a ratchet masquerade as a wall."),
      p(text: "**Done means executed.** The deepest trap is **specification-without-execution**: a typecheck and a `.contains()` grep are not consumers. Done means a real consumer **green by execution** plus a discriminating input that goes red when the behavior is wrong. Fluent, type-checking, grep-passing output is exactly what looks finished without running."),
      p(text: "A test's oracle needs an independent referent. **A merge-blocking test may compare a live repository population to a numeric literal only when the literal is grounded in a controlled fixture, an external or versioned authority, an explicit policy budget, or a monotone debt contract over a closed, independently discovered universe checked at identity grain.** A count copied from the current tree is a change detector: if automating its update collapses the test to `measure() == measure()`, it checked nothing. Completeness is an identity join, not a count equality."),
      p(text: "**Stop visibly; never conceal the deficit.** When the precise answer or the final construction is unavailable, stop visibly. Do not hide the deficit by widening, bypassing, or installing a second path; the concealment erases the only signal that the precise mechanism has a deficit. It takes four forms:"),
      ul(items: [
        li(text: "**Runtime uncertainty → typed refusal.** Substituting the superset (rerun everything, scan all keys) is the *absorbing fallback*: it conflates ⊤-as-answer with ⊤-as-ignorance and its cost scales with the corpus rather than the change. A failure arm refuses, never widens, and every degradation is typed, located and countable. Not this pattern: an over-approximation computed *as* the answer (a conservative selection that keeps work because omitting it was not justified), or a loud, budget-bounded interim fallback landing with its dissolution trigger. Failing to compute the answer is a different outcome from computing a conservative one, and contradictory input facts may require refusal rather than more work."),
        li(text: "**A refusal that fires → the line stops.** A toggle that proceeds as if a refusal had not fired is an *escape hatch*. The discipline is the **factory model**: a deficit stops the line and is analyzed before restart; the only second mode is an audit replay that ledgers deficits and reports rather than greens."),
        li(text: "**An authoring obstacle → root cause.** Routing around a parse error you do not understand, or a check that will not green, is the *workaround*. The concealed deficit is usually in the language layer, so **noticing you are implementing a workaround IS the line-stop signal.**"),
        li(text: "**A temporary deviation → operator approval outside the diff.** A dissolution condition describes how admitted debt ends; it does not authorize creating it. A scaffold's benefit is zero where the modeled path exists. → [scaffold admission doctrine](docs/plans/scaffold-admission-doctrine.md)."),
      ]),
      p(text: "**A diff landing a silent widen, fabricated default, uncounted degradation or escape hatch is a hard reject.**"),
      p(text: "Distinct from these, because it crosses a principal boundary: moving deferred cost onto someone else — customers, employees, neighbors, future maintainers — is **externalization**. The honest arms are to absorb and reserve for the risk, or to expose the transfer as a separately named and priced contract; keeping the old name, price or contract while someone else bears the burden is **externalized degradation**."),
    ]),
    claim_section(r: witness_one_interface, body: [
      p(text: "A claim's subject has a layer too. A witness's inputs are SUPPLIED VALUES at its boundary; deriving them re-executes production the claim is not about, once per claim — redundant work in a test. The tell: the claim's cost does not move when its assertions change. Reach is usually transitive (a fold over fifteen producers gives its claims their union), and its sharpest form is a production module importing `test.claim.*` (receipt gunbc#11457: 1,212,647 eval steps, approved twice). Supply the values; never buy the reach with a debt row."),
      p(text: "**The pairing obligation:** every supplied boundary owes ONE INHABITANCE CLAIM that the real producer emits the supplied shape on a bounded, representative real input, asserting the ROUTE and not only the answer, and SUPPLYING INPUTS MAY NEVER REMOVE THE LAST EXECUTION OF THE REAL PATH — deleting the integration must make a control fail. This proves that the real path inhabits the interface, not that every corpus member does; population coverage is a separate obligation when a cut needs it. A designed fixture can stay green after its world moves by reaching the same verdict another way; the inhabitance claim is what turns a supplied hypothesis into a reading."),
    ]),
  ], bs => bs))
}

// ── Changing the system ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

fn changing_blocks() -> List<MarkdownBlock> {
  concat([h2(text: "Changing the system")], flat_map([
    claim_section(r: conformance, body: [
      p(text: concat("A change touches concept domains — it caches, holds something exclusively, places work, restates an upstream fact — and for each the corpus already has a home module ", concat(ruling(r: conformance_needs_attention), ". The answer is three-valued:"))),
      ul(items: [
        li(text: "**Conforms.**"),
        li(text: "**Diverges with a stated reason** — admitted, and often correct."),
        li(text: "**Diverges with no reason** — the only red, because an unstated divergence forks the authority and its author was the one person who could cheaply say whether it was intended."),
      ]),
      p(text: "A two-valued reading loses the middle and either blocks every deliberate departure or admits every accidental one."),
      p(text: concat("A domain is added by adding a row with its home; the leasing row's authorization-pattern clause was added that way ", concat(ruling(r: authorization_pattern_is_a_selection), concat(". A domain whose home does not yet exist is a modeling obligation, not a row; a home that does not resolve is a citation defect; a home that owns only part of the row's claimed scope is a scope mismatch on the row. ", concat(see(r: single_authority), concat(" keeps naming and citation, ", concat(see(r: minimize_redundancy), " keeps copied work, and conformance owns *inhabitance*. Criterion keys are distinct (`gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_review_criteria` `review_criterion_identities_are_unique`) but not semantically exclusive, so two criteria may report one defect from two perspectives."))))))),
      p(text: "**On the ladder** a reviewer over a finished diff is *mitigation*. The climb: which domains a change touches is derivable before any worker runs — from the modules its plan imports and the vocabulary it introduces that a home already owns — so plan admission can name the homes in the brief and spawn only the reviewers the change reaches, leaving the roster as the safety net."),
      p(text: "The domains, each with what it owns, its boundaries and its existing authorities, are modeled in `gunbc.design_argument` `conformance_domains`; `gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_review_criteria` derives one reviewer from each, and the roster is rendered under *Reference* below."),
    ]),
    claim_section(r: consumption, body: [
      p(text: concat("Conformance asks whether a change inhabits existing models; **consumption asks the converse of every model it adds** ", concat(ruling(r: consumption_interrogated_separately), ". A typecheck and a grep are not consumers, but folded into other questions that let a diff add a well-shaped type, a row and a witness that the row exists while nothing read the row. A declaration nothing consumes is redundant work in its purest form."))),
      p(text: "For each added declaration, **name the consumer** (fold, entry point, realization, emitted artifact) **and the executing route**, or classify it: consumed in this change; consumed by a named later change (a declared frontier, with its trigger); or dangling — the only red, however well modeled. Tells: a `pub` declaration with no call site in the closure, a row no fold reads, a module nothing imports, a witness asserting existence rather than exercising behavior, consumption claimed in the PR body but absent from the diff."),
      p(text: concat("**The same law governs what a system runs on: a dependency is admitted by its consumer** ", concat(ruling(r: dependencies_are_admitted_by_their_consumer), ". Everything a system relies on or leaves enabled — a library or upstream service, a grant, a daemon or open port, a kernel module, a firmware behaviour, an onboard device or link — is admitted for a named consumer, and everything else is **not admitted**: switched off where it can be, and named as residue where it cannot. An enabled dependency nothing uses is the dangling red at run time: it costs to run, widens the surface where harm hides, and makes its failure modes integration work nobody chose. *e.g.* srv1's BMC graphics link served nothing, yet the host hung while the board's firmware-first error handler was taking that link's errors; switching the link off needed no settled account of the hang, only the absence of a consumer. Admission is held at the grain where the consumer exists (a board-wide default, or one host's exception) and recorded where the consumer is, never as a hand-kept allowlist beside it. Enabling is its own act with its own evidence: expose the dependency to a deliberately diverse sample, model each interaction it reveals, and admit it only where it behaves; a new interaction narrows admission again rather than becoming tolerated noise. Switching off is not masking: removing a dependency removes its failure modes, while running it with its errors hidden is concealment. Enumerating what one unit carries is bring-up exploration bounded to that unit, not a census. A layer may sit below this posture today, as hardware does while its admission is unmodeled; that is a stated standing with a next trigger, never a permissive default, and each layer moves toward the narrowest set its consumers need as convergence matures."))),
      p(text: "**On the ladder** the reviewer is *mitigation*. A declaration's consumers are already a fact of the namespace tree, so the unconsumed set is derivable at ingestion and plan admission can refuse a dangling set before any worker runs. Until then this is the one criterion that reads a change from its consumers inward."),
    ]),
    claim_section(r: replacement_migration, body: [
      p(text: "Editing X leaf-upward keeps its root alive while every intermediate state becomes load-bearing, and a surviving X is an **attractor** whose vocabulary keeps shaping decisions premised on an assumption scheduled to die."),
      p(text: concat("So the default is **delete-first**: uproot the root early, then fix forward minimally, solving each surfaced problem from first principles rather than restoring its old binding. **The deletion is an executing consumer check only over the population the run compiles**. Outside the required gate (",
        concat(documentary_ref_symbol_text(reference: required_gate_subjects_ref),
        ", the closures of the entries those rows emit, so nearly every root is outside it) a dependent can stop resolving while the required job reports SUCCESS, so **for a root outside the gate, enumerate the consumers by name before you delete** (start with `git grep` and source inspection to identify candidates; an empty text search is not proof that no semantic consumer exists). Report a concrete surviving dependency found that way; do not claim completeness, and do not hold the cut for a global inventory. What cannot break loudly is covered by a declared, bounded rung drop, never by silence. The same holds for references: a change that renames, moves or renumbers something moves every citation of it in the same change, comments included, so no stale reference is left behind."))),
      p(text: "Two carve-outs:"),
      ul(items: [
        li(text: "**A gap-intolerant boundary keeps the staged form:** Y in shadow, then one transition that switches the root and deletes X."),
        li(text: "**Where no Y can hold the boundary, X stays frozen:** no new investment, no new rows on its growth surfaces."),
      ]),
      p(text: "**Atomic describes the authority transition, not the effort:** X's authority ends in one motion, Y never falls back to, resolves through or derives from X (X may serve as an offline differential oracle), and the minimum Y preserves every required refusal."),
      p(text: concat("Frozen is the wrong state for a production-critical X whose replacement paused indefinitely, which is the v1 seed's situation: v1 is semantics-frozen, maintenance-active, under a PURPOSE test — a change is admitted when it serves the v2 self-host program. The authority is ",
        concat(documentary_ref_symbol_text(reference: v1_maintenance_standing_ref),
        ", which also records that an active-maintenance arm becomes absorbing if every proposal classifies as admissible, and that its vocabulary is enforced by review, not by a gate."))),
      p(text: "The full doctrine (operative loop, consumer-relative root, root-first cut search, terminal receipt): → [replacement migration doctrine](docs/plans/replacement-migration-doctrine.md)."),
    ]),
    claim_section(r: how_to_work, body: [
      ul(items: [
        li(text: "**Model:** search the concept graph before inventing vocabulary; invent or reuse on proven coincidence, never bare-alias; a finished stage is one fold (any residue is a named irreducible kernel or un-migrated modeling); model just-in-time and let the mark on the carrier be the authority, not a parallel ledger."),
        li(text: "**Intellectual sustainability:** do not spend future author, reviewer, operator or maintainer time for present convenience. Work expected to be thrown away is *presumed redundant*. The reviewer's test, whatever the author labelled: **will this artifact survive the terminal architecture substantially unchanged, and be consumed by it?** If not, presume scaffold and stop the merge; a missing dissolution condition makes that more severe, and adding one after review only makes the proposal eligible for a decision."),
        li(text: "**Tells, each a presumption:** a hand-authored workflow, deployment or migration script (out-of-band actuation); a second path beside a modeled route (parallel authority); bridge, shim, for now, temporary, until, later (deferred refactor); a model to be deleted whole when the real one lands; a hand-authored projection the model should generate; raw shell implementing semantics expressible in `.dag`; a broad wrapper around a type or modeling deficit; a condition whose terminal is rewrite this properly; a new artifact with no final consumer."),
        li(text: concat("**Rank on a funded multi-axis field** (", concat(see(r: selection_precedes_convergence), "). A 5ms step gets no pass for not being the 80s one; it might be a 5ns step. Hence **bare minimum cost**: a proven cost-shape defect — a copied accumulator, a quadratic fold — is *always fixed*, regardless of realized n, because n is small here is not time-stable."))),
        li(text: "**Denominate the benefit:** the deliverable is a *displaced cost* someone pays to remove; the lens or substrate is the mechanism. Priced in elegance, the work is unbounded — the purity trap, economic twin of the never-trap."),
        li(text: concat("**Proportionate evidence; a bad process is worse than no process** ", concat(ruling(r: bad_process_is_worse_than_no_process), ". Make the invalid state unwritable where possible. Repository-wide census and audit machinery is not a prerequisite for ordinary development: the census work, census-conditioned review requirements and narrower audit instruments without a concrete retained consumer are retired, removal owes no replacement instrument, and lens support becoming available does not revive them. Start with bounded `git grep`, source inspection and focused tests of the changed behavior; a search or observation names the exact command, revision, population inspected and what remains unknown, and a negative match is not a semantic absence proof. State material limitations without turning every unknown into a new gate: a missing global inventory is not, by itself, grounds to block a change, and a blocking review names a concrete correctness risk or an explicit requirement of the operation being changed. Where a change genuinely needs stronger evidence, seek the smallest evidence that resolves that question rather than reopening a corpus-wide measurement program. A measured number used as evidence names a reproducible producer when that measurement is worth its cost. **Preserve semantics, not ceremony:** compiler correctness, runtime refusal behavior and necessary execution tests remain requirements, retiring a census does not declare its old coverage achieved, and a lens with no executing consumer is evidence, not enforcement. Runtime, memory and disruption count against the cost a check displaces."))),
        li(text: concat("**Root-cause to the language layer** and fix related systems together; a local subsystem patch is the forked-logic trap (", concat(see(r: earliest_unjustified_boundary), "). *e.g.* one catamorphism `fold_node` serves every v2 stage, and #4699 dissolved `06_translate` from 4,912 to 3,973 lines by using it."))),
      ]),
    ]),
    claim_section(r: earliest_unjustified_boundary, body: [
      p(text: concat("This is how the root-cause rule is discharged ", concat(ruling(r: chain_re_derivation), ". A defect is observed at one link `c` of a dependency slice `a → b → c → d`. Patching `c` where it misbehaves, when the fault is upstream, encodes an undeclared fact about `a` or `b` — validation where construction was available, a second authority, redundant with the derivation it shadows — and the defect recurs at the next consumer of the same fact."))),
      p(text: "The method:"),
      ol(items: [
        li(text: "Identify the producer-to-consumer slice the defect sits in."),
        li(text: "For each link, state what it means, what it may assume, and what its owning contract and every relevant consumer (`d` included) establish that it owes."),
        li(text: "**Stop at the earliest boundary where the derivation cannot be justified** — a contract violated, missing, under-specified, duplicated, or insufficient for its consumer."),
        li(text: "Repair there: declare the missing assumption on its owning carrier, consolidate duplicate answers, and delete work no admitted consumer demands. Work required by the interface, another consumer or a safety obligation is not redundant because one consumer ignores it."),
        li(text: "Re-realize the downstream links rather than patching them; leave sound upstream links alone."),
        li(text: "Use measurement to falsify or confirm the derivation, never to choose the repair."),
      ]),
      p(text: "If the earliest unjustified boundary is `c`, the local fix *is* the root-cause repair. *e.g.* resolve once rebuilt corpus-wide state per module, and the repair was upstream: one shared `ResolutionContext` per native ingest in `v2.compiler.compile`. By contrast, when the emitter's own tail-call lowering was the earliest unjustified boundary, the local repair was the right one."),
      p(text: "**Measurement is the adversary, not the selector.** It locates the failing route, prioritizes the slice and confirms the old computation is gone; a sound reading states beforehand which multiplicity collapses, which route disappears and which outputs stay equal, and a contradiction from a reconciled instrument falsifies the claim (a failed reconciliation falsifies the instrument). But a number underdetermines the chain. Targeting one axis (make resolve 10% faster) yields a cache, batch, pool or knob at the measured link that moves cost rather than removing it; such a mechanism is right only when derived from the demand graph, a complete identity and an admitted provider (`std.materialization_ladder`)."),
      ul(items: [
        li(text: "**Owned components get opened.** An external upstream stays a black box with an honestly modeled contract; an owned component whose contract is missing, violated, duplicated or insufficient must be opened."),
        li(text: concat("**No level is presumed correct**, including the structure the slice sits in (", concat(see(r: epistemic_humility), " turned inward). Touching code is taking responsibility for it."))),
        li(text: "**There are no special layers.** The seed, the emitter or a load-bearing stage raises the evidence bar for landing, not an exemption from the read."),
      ]),
      p(text: "The tells: a symptom-link fix with no statement of why that link is the earliest unjustified boundary; a guard for a state earlier links could have made unwritable; a second fix to one link in a short time; a percentage on one axis as justification; knowing *that* `c` is wrong without being able to say what `a` and `b` established — the line-stop. The class is `gunbc.recurring_failure_mode::symptom_link_patched_without_the_earliest_unjustified_boundary`."),
    ]),
    claim_section(r: root_generalization, body: [
      p(text: concat("Chain re-derivation fixes depth: it finds the earliest unjustified link of one slice. A rule repaired correctly at that link can still live in sibling leaves, and the repair then reaches one of them ", concat(ruling(r: root_generalization_closes_breadth), ". Root generalization closes breadth, and its formal core is anti-unification, Plotkin's least general generalization (G. D. Plotkin, \"A note on inductive generalization\", Machine Intelligence 5, 1970): the most specific rule of which every observed case is an instance. A leaf that answers one case beside leaves that answer its siblings is a fork, consolidated later at the price of every leaf written in between."))),
      ol(items: [
        li(text: "**Detect.** A new case seems to need new code: is it another instance of what existing leaves already do? The trigger is the second local implementation of one rule."),
        li(text: "**Collect** the known and discoverable sibling implementations of the rule, layer by layer: sibling arms, callers, helpers, v1 and v2, stages and targets. Start with cited grep and source inspection and state what was inspected; the fork list is a candidate set, not a mechanically complete census. State the unknowns; they are not a gate. Report a concrete surviving sibling when one is found, and never trigger an unbounded instrument to rule the rest out."),
        li(text: concat("**Generalize** to the least general rule that covers them all, placed at the earliest unjustified boundary. Too general a rule forbids or admits what no leaf did (", concat(see(r: epistemic_humility), ")."))),
        li(text: concat("**Delete the leaves** (", concat(see(r: replacement_migration), "). A generalization beside surviving leaves is a second path."))),
        li(text: "**Repeat** while the generalizations themselves form a family."),
      ]),
      p(text: "Families that grew leaf by leaf: alias identity at type-compatibility call sites, per-trait bound pipelines, and qualified-call resolution; receipts and positive specimens are in the ruling and the reviewer brief. The seven submit deadlines in the Google example are a candidate family the same question applies to."),
      p(text: concat("The vertical half is `gunbc.recurring_failure_mode::symptom_link_patched_without_the_earliest_unjustified_boundary`; the horizontal half is `gunbc.recurring_failure_mode::a_rule_is_applied_at_the_site_that_prompted_it_and_not_its_sibling`, whose recognition rule is addressed to the author, who stops when the prompting instance is fixed. So the same question is asked of the reviewer, for every domain, by this claim's review part. A repeated pattern is rejected until the author answers why it should not be generalized (", concat(see(r: conformance), concat(", diverges with a stated reason) or that it should be but not now, naming the trigger and follow-up (", concat(see(r: guarantee_ladder), ", no untracked stall); the reviewer accepts or rejects that answer, and no answer is a reject."))))),
    ]),
    claim_section(r: continuous_generalization, body: [
      p(text: concat("Root generalization closes a family once its leaves are visible; continuous generalization keeps unfinished integration visible until it is closed ", concat(ruling(r: continuous_generalization_is_two_sided), ". Use cases begin specific, and that is admitted; an individual change may leave integration unfinished."))),
      p(text: "**Reconciliation is two-sided.** A newcomer accounts for the incumbent's valid obligations, and the incumbent for the newcomer's valid requirements; neither has authority because it arrived first or is the current task. The new case is another instance of an existing rule, or exposes a missing dimension or mistaken assumption in it, or belongs to a genuinely different concept. Forcing it into a model that cannot express it (a sentinel value, flag or adapter branch) and bypassing a model that could (a second path) are both forks. Apply this through the affected dependency slice and its known sibling consumers, and generalize only as far as the combined requirements justify. Namespace views locate the work; **namespace ancestry does not determine semantic ownership.**"),
      p(text: "**An unfinished reconciliation is recorded, not remembered.** It lives in its existing owning record or in `gunbc.integration_concern`, which separates a suspected fork (two or more distinct authorities answering one question) from a suspected capability gap (one model that cannot express a concrete requirement), and an affected location from a semantic home. Recording neither satisfies an obligation nor authorizes a contract or safety deviation. A sighting of a known concern updates its row rather than adding one. A concern closes as integrated, with the integrated revision, the superseded declarations cited at the revision where they existed (so deletion does not break the record) and retained execution results; as distinct, with a reason scoped to what was examined; or as withdrawn, with a reason. A completed work item, a merged abstraction or a shorter ledger is not evidence of integration."),
      p(text: "Conformance stays three-valued; what is being done about an inadequate model is a concern's disposition, not a conformance answer. Concerns are read by relevance to a change — the modules it changes and the existing models it references — and browsed by subtree. Discovery is not exhaustive and nothing here is a census: a reviewer may require a disposition for a concern that was raised, never proof that nothing else was noticed."),
    ]),
  ], bs => bs))
}

// ── Self-hosting ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

fn self_hosting_blocks() -> List<MarkdownBlock> {
  concat([h2(text: "The method applied to the compiler")], claim_section(r: self_hosting, body: [
    p(text: concat("The compiler is a pure transform, analyzable by its own lenses. The `.dag` graph is the truth and Rust is one realization — a seed that shrinks toward zero. v2 emits its own sources, proven **by execution**: the emitted module compiles and holds its own `.dag` contract on a discriminating corpus ", concat(ruling(r: v2_better_than_the_seed), ". The seed is a regression oracle, never a specification: a divergence is either a *v2 regression* (a defect) or a *seed defect* v2 does not carry forward and records as a correction. The seed reading something one way is evidence of what v2 must not lose, never by itself why v2 reads it that way — so neither a byte-identical fixed point nor behavioral equivalence is the goal; both would cement the seed's warts."))),
    p(text: "The seed shrinks across a **typed self-host frontier**: each module is *self-emitted* (green by execution) or *seed-retained*, and a retained module is a declared row with reason and migration trigger, never a silent escape hatch. Its tests are data; its ontology dissolves into `std/`."),
    p(text: "Language design is normally locked by cost: a new check means owning a compiler fork, a new language means an adoption problem. Here a wall is a **row** (no fork), and because the substrate is medium-agnostic, that row applies *on top of an existing language* (no adoption): a domain's bug class can be made extinct in Rust or TypeScript without forking their compilers. It is sound exactly where ingest is lossless and fail-closed where it is not — any language, with a typed honesty boundary about where the wall holds. This is convention-to-necessity at the meta-level, bounded by decidability and by displaced cost, not elegance."),
  ]))
}

// ── Reference ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Not part of the argument: rosters and the operational contract, rendered from their carriers.

// A DOMAIN RENDERS AS ITS OWN SUBSECTION, NOT ONE BULLET. The row is a record -- what the domain
// owns, what it deliberately leaves to a neighbour, and its homes grouped by what they do.
fn conformance_home_group_item(g: ConformanceHomeGroup) -> MarkdownListItem {
  li_blocks(blocks: [
    p(text: join(["**", g.label, "** — ", g.purpose], "")),
    ul(items: map(conformance_home_list(h: g.homes), h => li(text: concat("`", concat(review_home_text(h: h), "`"))))),
  ])
}

fn conformance_boundary_blocks(d: ConformanceDomain) -> List<MarkdownBlock> {
  if count(d.boundaries) == 1 {
    map(d.boundaries, b => p(text: concat("**Boundary.** ", b)))
  } else {
    [p(text: "**Boundaries.**"), ul(items: map(d.boundaries, b => li(text: b)))]
  }
}

fn conformance_domain_section(d: ConformanceDomain) -> List<MarkdownBlock> {
  concat(
    concat(
      [h3(text: d.label), p(text: concat("**Owns.** ", d.owns))],
      conformance_boundary_blocks(d: d),
    ),
    [
      p(text: "**Existing authorities**"),
      ul(items: map(conformance_domain_home_groups(d: d), g => conformance_home_group_item(g: g))),
    ],
  )
}

fn conformance_reference_blocks() -> List<MarkdownBlock> {
  concat([
    h2(text: "Reference: conformance domains"),
    p(text: concat("The roster ", concat(see(r: conformance), " reviews against, from `gunbc.design_argument` `conformance_domains`. Each domain states what part of a change it **owns**, a nearby responsibility it leaves to another **boundary**, and the **existing authorities** that already carry its model; a conforming change reuses or extends those rather than introducing a parallel representation."))),
  ], flat_map(conformance_domains, d => conformance_domain_section(d: d)))
}

// THE CI CONTRACT. A ruling whose subject is the job roster or the gate governs this function
// (gunbc.design_ruling DesignRuling governs), which is why it has a stable name.
fn ci_contract_blocks() -> List<MarkdownBlock> {
  [
    h2(text: ci_contract_heading),
    p(text: "The CI contract. This part is `gunbc.design_document::ci_contract_blocks`. From a bare clone, start at `gunbc.contributor_onboarding_path` (combined view: `gunbc.instruments.docs_projection_gate` `regen`): the walked path to a first landed change, the CLIs, local checks, hooks and regeneration, and a reading order for this document."),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: concat("**CI is one required job.** `gunbc.witness.compiler_gate_workflow` emits `.github/workflows/witnesses.yml`; the required context is the job `", concat(required_ci_context_name as String, concat("`, run on the fleet for every pull request and merge_group ", concat(ruling(r: v1_withdrawn_one_required_job), ". It builds the seed, then runs one `gunbc test <label>` step per row of `gunbc.emitted_subject_build_gate` `emitted_subject_build_rows` (today `//gunbc/instruments:self-host` and `//gunbc/instruments:v2-native-cli`): the seed emits a v2 closure, cargo builds it fresh, and the built binary runs against its controls. A fork pull request has no required check — unobserved, not excused."))))),
      li(text: concat("**A non-required job repairs two generated populations and judges nothing.** `gunbc.heal_workflow` runs on same-repository pull requests that touch the generation inputs, converges the stage0 mirrors to the seed's own fixed point and rewrites the seed-build artifacts, and `gunbc.heal_publisher_workflow` pushes the result as one bot commit, compare-and-set on the head, with a receipt naming each artifact. A fork pull request gets a notice, and a non-converging round pushes nothing. `gunbc.drift_watch_workflow` runs on a schedule, reads the default branch and fails with one typed, located finding per drifted workflow, runner sudoers, githook, `.gitignore`, `.gitattributes` or fci1 context, each naming its remedy (`gunbc.tier_o_artifact_emit` `tier_o_artifacts`); it writes nothing. Neither is a required context and neither refuses a merge; every other generated artifact is left to its author (`gunbc.rung_drop` `v1_required_lanes_withdrawn`); the two added jobs are recorded at ", concat(ruling(r: regeneration_jobs_added_to_roster), "."))),
      li(text: concat("**The memory envelope is part of the verdict** ", concat(ruling(r: memory_envelope_is_part_of_the_verdict), ". Each subject step refuses a run no cgroup `memory.max` bounds, prints a memory receipt at exit, and refuses on any OOM kill or any swap whatever the producer answered (`gunbc.host.memory_envelope`; slot shape `gunbc.runner.runner_slot_desired`). Three authorities, kept apart: the slot says what is admitted; the exit verdict reads the two terminal readings a swapless slot can produce; and liveness — a working set over memory.high can hold under the throttle below memory.max indefinitely, with no OOM kill and no swap — is observed while the run executes, from outside it (`gunbc.host.memory_stall_refusal` `supervised_subject_verdict`; class `gunbc.recurring_failure_mode::swap_zero_read_as_a_thrash_refusal`)."))),
      li(text: concat("**What a green `", concat(required_ci_context_name as String, "` does not say.** Nothing on the required path evaluates a witness, refuses a module outside the two emitted closures, runs clippy or the v1 unit tests, or compares a generated artifact — this document included — with its authority. That loss is one declared drop, `gunbc.rung_drop` `v1_required_lanes_withdrawn`, whose trigger is the native compiler judging the population the drop declares. A new verdict joins the gate as a row judged by a binary built from the seed's emission, never as a lane handing the corpus to the v1 interpreter."))),
      li(text: concat("**The job roster and its budgets are closed to growth** ", concat(ruling(r: job_roster_closed_to_growth), concat(" ", concat(ruling(r: ci_job_budgets_closed_to_growth), ": adding a job needs operator sign-off, and the operator has banned any increase to a CI time or memory budget without their explicit sign-off or approval, and has stated that memory budgets are to be lowered, not raised. The second ruling row holds the operator's words and, labelled separately, the interim interpretation of what counts as a time-budget increase. The reasoning an author owes for a new job is stated at `witness_floor_lane_jobs`."))))),
      li(text: concat("**An instrument is a row, not a flag** ", concat(ruling(r: instrument_is_a_row_not_a_flag), ". Investigation does not require one (see *Proportionate evidence* under the how-to-work claim). When a measurement warrants an instrument, a `gunbc test <label>` address is a row in `gunbc.instrument_targets` bound through `gunbc.target_binding` `TargetProducer`, never a flag on `claim_executor`. Exit 0 means the observation held, 1 that it did not, 2 that there was no observation (an unknown label refuses with 2). A new wide instrument must first name its real consumer, discriminator and acceptable cost; required completeness is never certified by grep."))),
      li(text: concat("**Instrument measurements queue per dispatch, not one behind all** ", concat(ruling(r: instrument_measurement_queues_run_in_parallel), ". `gunbc.instruments.instrument_dispatch_workflow` `instrument_dispatch_concurrency_group` keys the concurrency group on the dispatch ref plus the dispatch label, so unrelated measurements proceed at once while one measurement on one ref cannot run twice against itself; the queue is `max` and cancellation stays off, so a pending run is never dropped. Isolation between runs on the shared self-hosted pool is the SLOT's, not the queue's: `gunbc.runner.runner_slot_desired` declares the per-slot shape and `gunbc.host.memory_envelope` enforces it. That is what isolation means for an instrument run, not a claim that the live fleet carries it: live runner units drift from the declared shape (`gunbc.recurring_failure_mode::the_live_runner_unit_memory_shape_drifts_from_the_declared_slot`, whose next-rung trigger is a scheduled live read of every fleet slot refusing on drift), so until that trigger runs, parallel measurements rest on a declared authority that is not fully installed."))),
    ]),
    h2(text: ledgers_heading),
    p(text: "Recurring failure modes live one file per class under `dag/gunbc/recurring_failure_mode/` (`gunbc.recurring_failure_mode`), each with its recognition rule and receipts. They are a ledger, not a consequence, so they are not indexed here: an index in this file would regenerate it on every append."),
  ]
}

fn design_blocks() -> List<MarkdownBlock> {
  flat_map([
    preamble_blocks(),
    purpose_blocks(),
    method_blocks(),
    worked_examples_blocks(),
    meaning_blocks(),
    facts_blocks(),
    representation_blocks(),
    composition_blocks(),
    guarantees_blocks(),
    changing_blocks(),
    self_hosting_blocks(),
    conformance_reference_blocks(),
    ci_contract_blocks(),
  ], bs => bs)
}

fn design_document() -> MarkdownDocument {
  MarkdownDocument { blocks: design_blocks() }
}

fn expected_design_md() -> String {
  serialize_markdown_source(doc: design_document())
}

fn design_drifted(committed: String) -> Bool {
  !(committed == expected_design_md())
}
